      <p class="infographic-caption">Figure 1: Major historical milestones shaping global Daylight Saving Time adoption.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 2 -->
  <section id="early-proposals" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Early Proposals">
    <h2>2. 1784–1907: Benjamin Franklin, Hudson &amp; Willett</h2>
    <p>
      The earliest conceptual precursor to DST dates back to 1784, when Benjamin Franklin authored a satirical essay in the <em>Journal de Paris</em>. Franklin calculated that 100,000 Parisian families burning candles at night could save 64 million pounds of candle wax annually by waking up at sunrise.
    </p>
    <p>
      In 1895, New Zealand entomologist George Hudson presented a formal scientific paper proposing a 2-hour seasonal clock adjustment to increase post-work evening daylight for collecting insects.
    </p>
    <p>
      In 1907, British builder William Willett self-funded a campaign publishing <em>The Waste of Daylight</em>, urging Parliament to advance clocks by 80 minutes across four April Sundays to extend evening golf hours.
    </p>
    <p>
      To understand how these historical shifts compare with core timekeeping definitions, consult our guides on <a href="/what-is-daylight-saving-time/">what is Daylight Saving Time</a>, <a href="/what-is-gmt/">what is GMT</a>, and <a href="/what-is-est/">what is EST</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 3 -->
  <section id="world-war-one" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="World War I Adoption">
    <h2>3. World War I (1916): German Adoption &amp; Allied Responses</h2>
    <p>
      World War I converted DST from an academic proposal into national energy policy. On April 30, 1916, the German Empire enacted DST to reduce lighting energy demand and conserve coal for war production.
    </p>
    <p>
      Great Britain introduced British Summer Time (<a href="/bst-to-est/">BST</a>) on May 21, 1916. The United States enacted national DST in March 1918 under the Standard Time Act.
    </p>
    <p>
      For broader historical context, see our overview on <a href="/why-dst-exists/">why DST exists</a> and our overall <a href="/daylight-saving-time-explained/">Daylight Saving Time explained</a> guide.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 4 -->
  <section id="interwar-war-time" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Interwar War Time">
    <h2>4. WWII "War Time" &amp; Post-War Transportation Chaos</h2>
    <p>
      After WWI, intense lobbying by agricultural groups led US Congress to repeal federal DST in 1919 over President Woodrow Wilson's veto.
    </p>
    <p>
      During World War II, President Franklin D. Roosevelt instituted year-round DST in February 1942, designated as "War Time" (e.g., Eastern War Time or EWT). When federal regulations lapsed after WWII in 1945, individual cities and states established conflicting local clock change schedules, creating significant operational disruption for airlines, railroads, and radio broadcasters.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 5 -->
  <section id="uniform-time-act" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="The Uniform Time Act">
    <h2>5. The Uniform Time Act of 1966</h2>
    <p>
      To eliminate post-war timekeeping confusion, US Congress passed the <strong>Uniform Time Act of 1966</strong>. The act established:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Standardized federal transition dates: starting the last Sunday of April and ending the last Sunday of October.</li>
      <li>State opt-out provisions: allowing states to opt out of DST via state legislation, provided the entire state remained on standard time. Arizona and Hawaii utilized this opt-out provision. Learn more in our <a href="/usa-time-zones/">USA time zones guide</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 6 -->
  <section id="seventies-crisis" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="The 1970s Energy Crisis">
    <h2>6. The 1970s Energy Crisis &amp; Permanent DST Experiment</h2>
    <p>
      In response to the 1973 OPEC oil embargo, President Richard Nixon signed the Emergency Daylight Saving Time Energy Conservation Act, instituting permanent year-round DST starting January 6, 1974.
    </p>
    <p>
      Public support quickly evaporated during winter months when children had to commute to school in total darkness, leading to an increase in morning traffic accidents. Congress repealed the emergency act in October 1974, restoring seasonal winter standard time.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 7 -->
  <section id="energy-policy-act" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Energy Policy Act of 2005">
    <h2>7. The Energy Policy Act of 2005 Extension</h2>
    <p>
      The modern US schedule was established under the <strong>Energy Policy Act of 2005</strong>, signed by President George W. Bush. Effective in 2007, the act extended DST by four weeks:
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Era / Act</th>
            <th>Spring Start Date</th>
            <th>Autumn End Date</th>
            <th>Total DST Weeks</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Uniform Time Act (1966)</strong></td>
            <td>Last Sunday in April</td>
            <td>Last Sunday in October</td>
            <td>~26 Weeks</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Energy Policy Act (2005)</strong></td>
            <td>Second Sunday in March</td>
            <td>First Sunday in November</td>
            <td>~34 Weeks</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 8 -->
  <section id="global-evolution" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Global History">
    <h2>8. Global History: Europe, Russia, Asia &amp; Latin America</h2>
    <p>
      International adoption of DST has undergone significant historical shifts:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>European Union</strong>: Fully synchronized Summer Time start and end dates across all member nations in 1996. Check details in our <a href="/countries-that-use-dst/">countries that use DST guide</a> and <a href="/dst-start-end-dates/">DST start and end dates</a> page.</li>
      <li><strong>Russia</strong>: Tested permanent DST in 2011, then switched permanently to winter standard time in 2014. View non-DST history in our <a href="/countries-without-dst/">countries without DST guide</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>China</strong>: Observed DST from 1986 to 1991 before abandoning it to maintain a unified national timezone.</li>
      <li><strong>Australia</strong>: Introduced DST during WWI and WWII, standardizing state rules in 1971. Read more in our <a href="/australia-time-zones/">Australia time zones guide</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 9 -->
  <section id="historical-matrix" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Chronological Era Matrix">
    <h2>9. Chronological Era Matrix (1784 to Present)</h2>
    <p>
      Review historical legislative shifts across different timekeeping eras:
    </p>
    <p>
      For technical server configurations and historical timestamp conversions, consult our guides on <a href="/unix-time-explained/">Unix time explained</a> and <a href="/iso-8601-date-time-format/">ISO 8601 syntax</a>. Explore economic impact studies in our <a href="/business-impact-of-dst/">business impact of DST guide</a> and multi-year projections in our <a href="/dst-calendar/">DST calendar guide</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 10: FAQ SECTION -->
  <section id="faq-section" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Frequently Asked Questions">
    <h2>10. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
    <div class="faq-list">
      {meta.faqs.map((faq) => (
        <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem; padding: 1rem; background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px;">
          <h3 style="margin-top: 0; font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--text);">{faq.q}</h3>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 0; color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.6;">{faq.a}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 11: RECOMMENDATIONS & WIDGETS -->
  <section id="recommendations" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Related Converters and Widgets">
    <h2>11. Interactive Tools &amp; Related Guides</h2>
    <p>
      Calculate time zone conversions across historical and modern offsets:
    </p>
    
    <RelatedConvertersWidget slug={slug} />
    <UsersAlsoVisitWidget slug={slug} />
    <LearnMoreWidget slug={slug} />
    <CountryCityWidget slug={slug} />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

